<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Find a lab and its units; choose one value per person; convert units; form bins
and flags; measure change; and pool summaries and a model across two connections.
</div>

**You need:** the packages, downloaded helpers and two loaded resources in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start), plus the recipe basics
from [chapter 6](book-06-events.html). Start a fresh R session in `book-work`.
Run the sections in order: each uses objects defined immediately above it.

These are **public synthetic Synthea27Nj patients**, from the pinned Eunomia
archive used by the numeric validation loader. There are 28 fictional people,
not 27. GiBleed is the other practice resource and has no populated numeric
measurement values. All results below were executed with dsOMOP 2.10.4.

## Connect and find a lab

The shared connection file selects the driver, URL, project and resource names.
Selecting `numeric` switches this lesson to Synthea without changing those settings.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
practice <- book_connect("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

Search the vocabulary first. A *concept* identifies what was measured; it does
not identify a patient. Hemoglobin is the vocabulary's spelling of haemoglobin.

```{r find-lab}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Hemoglobin", conns = conns)
knitr::kable(head(hits$pooled[c("concept_name", "domain_id", "vocabulary_id")], 6))
```

Select the blood mass-concentration concept deliberately. Other hemoglobin
concepts can describe a different test. IDs in code select public vocabulary;
shown tables use their names.

```{r chosen-lab}
lab_id <- 3000963L
chosen <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(lab_id, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(chosen$pooled[c("concept_name", "domain_id")])
```

Now ask which units were recorded for this concept. This is a checked aggregate,
not a download of measurement rows.

```{r recorded-units}
unit_counts <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id",
  concept_id = lab_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
unit_counts$pooled
```

The counts describe measurements, not distinct people. This source has 108
hemoglobin readings recorded in grams per deciliter (g/dL).

**Exercise.** Look up the name of unit 8713. Does a vocabulary lookup prove that
any patient has a result in that unit?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r units-exercise}
unit_names <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(8713L, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(unit_names$pooled["concept_name"])
```

No. The lookup describes the unit; the preceding aggregate establishes recorded use.

</details>

## Choose one value per person

An *event* selects records. Here it selects one lab and one recorded unit.
A unit filter excludes other units; it does not convert them.

```{r lab-event}
lab <- omop_event("measurement", concepts = lab_id, value = "value_as_number",
  where = omop_where(unit_concept_id == 8713L))
class(lab)
```

A *reducer* turns repeated readings into one number. First and last use the
record dates (then source and record key for ties), whereas min and max use the
size of the value. These reducers skip missing values by default.

```{r first-last}
first <- omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("first_value"))
last <- omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("last_value"))
class(first)
```

The mean weights each available reading equally **within** a person. Min and
max show that person's observed range, not a clinical reference range.

```{r mean-extremes}
average <- omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("mean"))
minimum <- omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("min"))
maximum <- omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("max"))
class(average)
```

Omitting the window on these advanced reducers uses all recorded history.
Assign a wide table, with one private row per person. Only its symbol returns.

```{r assign-values}
values <- omop_recipe(variables = list(first = first, last = last,
  mean = average, minimum = minimum, maximum = maximum),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(values, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(values, out = c(people = "D_labs"), conns = conns))
```

Ask for an approved summary of the first values. All patient-level numbers
remain on the server; the table below contains summary statistics only.

```{r first-summary}
first_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_labs$first", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(first_summary))
```

<details><summary>Full aggregate response</summary>

```{r first-full}
first_summary
```

</details>

Compare the last-value distribution with the first-value distribution.
A change in these summaries alone does not describe each individual's change.

```{r last-summary}
last_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_labs$last", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(last_summary))
```

The next mean gives each person equal weight, regardless of their number of
readings. It is not the mean over all measurement rows.

```{r mean-summary}
mean_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_labs$mean", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(mean_summary))
```

**Exercise 1.** Summarise the minimum and maximum columns. Why can first differ
from minimum?

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r extremes-exercise}
minimum_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_labs$minimum", datasources = conns)
maximum_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_labs$maximum", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(rbind(transform(book_summary(minimum_summary), Column = "minimum"),
                  transform(book_summary(maximum_summary), Column = "maximum")))
```

First is earliest in time; minimum is smallest in value. They answer different questions.

</details>

**Exercise 2.** Would a person with no eligible lab have first = 0?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

No. First, last, mean, min and max are missing (`NA`) when no eligible nonmissing
values exist. Zero would claim a measured value. The person-grain table still
includes that person; this particular dataset has hemoglobin for everyone.

</details>

## Anchor a value and estimate a slope

An *index date* is the reference date for each study episode. For this teaching
example, choose seven days after each person's first lab. The offset makes it
clear that “at index” can use a nearby measurement rather than an exact-date match.

```{r index-definition}
index <- omop_index(lab, offset = 7L)
class(index)
```

Tolerance `c(-7, 0)` includes the preceding seven days and index day, never a
later reading. The shortcut selects the closest eligible nonmissing value.

```{r index-recipe}
indexed <- omop_study(index, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  at_index = omop_value_at_index("measurement", lab_id,
    tolerance = c(-7, 0), unit = 8713L)))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(indexed, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(indexed, out = c(baseline = "D_lab_index"), conns = conns))
```

Read its summary. If no measurement falls inside the tolerance the value is
missing, even when that person has measurements elsewhere in their history.

```{r index-summary}
index_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_index$at_index", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(index_summary))
```

A *slope* fits a straight line to value against date within each person. It uses
all eligible readings, unlike first-to-last subtraction. The unit here is g/dL
**per day**. At least two distinct measurement dates are needed.

```{r slope-recipe}
slopes <- omop_recipe(variables = list(per_day = omop_variable(event = lab,
  reducer = omop_reduce("slope"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(slopes, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(slopes, out = c(people = "D_lab_slopes"), conns = conns))
```

Summarise the slopes, not the private fitted lines.

```{r slope-summary}
slope_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_slopes$per_day", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(slope_summary))
```

**Exercise.** Express the slopes per 365 days and summarise them.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r slope-exercise}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_lab_slopes", per_365_days = 365 * per_day,
  newobj = "D_lab_annual", conns = conns))
annual_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_annual$per_365_days", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(annual_summary))
```

This rescales the same fitted slopes. It does not add observations or forecast
any person's next result. The fixed 365-day interval is explicit.

</details>

## Declare a common unit

To pool measurements, first align their meaning and units. Look up the target
unit grams per liter (g/L).

```{r target-unit}
target_unit <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(8636L, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(target_unit$pooled["concept_name"])
```

Declare the conversion: g/dL multiplied by ten becomes g/L. Rows already in
g/L retain their value. Other units become missing by default; the server never
guesses a conversion. `unknown = "exclude"` would drop those rows instead.

```{r conversion-event}
lab_gl <- omop_event("measurement", concepts = lab_id,
  unit = omop_unit(8636L, convert = list("8713" = c(factor = 10, offset = 0))))
class(lab_gl)
```

The conversion precedes predicates and reducers, and also transforms recorded
reference bounds. This source only has g/dL for this lab; we are converting its
actual values, not inventing a mixed-unit source.

```{r conversion-recipe}
converted <- omop_recipe(variables = list(mean_gl = omop_variable(event = lab_gl,
  reducer = omop_reduce("mean"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(converted, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(converted, out = c(people = "D_lab_gl"), conns = conns))
```

**Exercise.** Summarise the converted means. How should they compare with the
g/dL summary above?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r conversion-exercise}
conversion_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_gl$mean_gl", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(conversion_summary))
```

Every displayed quantile and the mean is ten times its earlier value. Use the
same target unit and declared conversion map at every site before pooling.

</details>

## Form bins and high or low flags

A *bin* groups values into an interval. Apply it to each person's mean, privately.
These are arbitrary teaching cutoffs, **not clinical reference limits**.

```{r lab-bins}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_labs",
  band = cut(mean, breaks = c(0, 14, 15, 100),
    labels = c("below 14", "14 to below 15", "15 or above"), right = FALSE),
  high = ifelse(mean >= 15, 1L, 0L), low = ifelse(mean < 14, 1L, 0L),
  newobj = "D_lab_flags", conns = conns))
```

Left boundaries are included; right boundaries are excluded. Values outside the
declared range and missing means have a missing bin. Release the bin counts:

```{r bin-counts}
bin_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_lab_flags$band", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(bin_counts))
```

These nonzero cells meet the release threshold. Smaller cells may cause the
whole table to be withheld. A refusal is not a zero count.

The dedicated `abnormal_high` and `abnormal_low` reducers instead compare each
reading with its own recorded `range_high` or `range_low` and count readings
outside the bounds. Synthea27Nj has **no recorded reference bounds**. Run them
to learn why a zero abnormal count does not establish normal results.

```{r range-recipe}
ranges <- omop_recipe(variables = list(
  high_count = omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("abnormal_high")),
  low_count = omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce("abnormal_low"))),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(ranges, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(ranges, out = c(people = "D_lab_ranges"), conns = conns))
```

Turn those counts into person-level flags. Both summaries below show zeros:
there were no comparable bounds, so no recorded abnormalities were counted.

```{r range-flags}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_lab_ranges",
  recorded_high = ifelse(high_count > 0, 1L, 0L),
  recorded_low = ifelse(low_count > 0, 1L, 0L),
  newobj = "D_recorded_flags", conns = conns))
recorded_high <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_recorded_flags$recorded_high", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
recorded_low <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_recorded_flags$recorded_low", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(rbind(transform(book_counts(recorded_high), Flag = "recorded high"),
                  transform(book_counts(recorded_low), Flag = "recorded low")))
```

**Exercise.** Summarise the two flags based on the explicit teaching cutoffs.
Why do they differ from the recorded-range flags?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r flags-exercise}
high_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_lab_flags$high", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
low_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_lab_flags$low", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(rbind(transform(book_counts(high_counts), Flag = "mean at least 15"),
                  transform(book_counts(low_counts), Flag = "mean below 14")))
```

The explicit flags compare a person's mean with declared cutoffs. The dedicated
reducers compare individual readings with their own source bounds, which are
absent here. Neither result justifies a clinical interpretation of these synthetic data.

</details>

## Calculate change from first to last

Define *change* as last minus first, in g/dL. Subtract on the server so each pair
belongs to the same person. This is a difference, not a rate per day.

```{r change}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_labs", change = last - first,
  newobj = "D_lab_change", conns = conns))
```

A positive value means the last reading was larger. Request only its summary:

```{r change-summary}
change_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_change$change", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(change_summary))
```

With only one eligible reading, first and last coincide and change is zero;
that does not establish a stable trajectory. Missing endpoints give missing change.

**Exercise.** Calculate each person's observed spread, maximum minus minimum.
Is it the same as change?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r spread-exercise}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_labs", spread = maximum - minimum,
  newobj = "D_lab_spread", conns = conns))
spread_summary <- dsBaseClient::ds.summary("D_lab_spread$spread", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_summary(spread_summary))
```

Spread cannot be negative and ignores ordering. Change preserves the direction
between the first and last readings, but ignores the intervening trajectory.

</details>

## Pool across two connections

This section is a **local DSLite exercise**, even if the earlier sections used
Opal. The helper creates temporary, disjoint copies of the public Synthea data:
14 people in `north`, 14 in `south`. It preserves their measurements and units,
then logs into two actual DSLite servers. It never partitions a live Opal dataset.
A real multisite analysis instead supplies its authorised connections and must
establish that populations do not overlap.

```{r two-sites}
pair <- book_numeric_pair()
two_conns <- pair$conns
names(two_conns)
```

Run the identical native recipe at both sites. A lab column has the same meaning,
unit, missing-value rule and person-level grain at each site.

```{r two-site-recipe}
stopifnot(recipe_explain(values, conns = two_conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(values, out = c(people = "D_pool_labs"), conns = two_conns))
```

`ds.mean` combines nonmissing observations across sites. Here each observation
is one person's mean lab value, so each person receives equal weight.

```{r pooled-mean}
pooled_mean <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_pool_labs$mean", type = "combine", datasources = two_conns)
pooled_mean
```

`ds.quantileMean` averages site quantiles with site-size weights. It is an
estimate of the combined distribution, **not the exact quantiles of concatenated
patient values**. Its mean agrees with the pooled mean here because all 28
person-level means are present. Missing data require care with denominators.

```{r pooled-quantiles}
pooled_quantiles <- dsBaseClient::ds.quantileMean("D_pool_labs$mean",
  type = "combine", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(data.frame(Statistic = names(pooled_quantiles), Value = as.numeric(pooled_quantiles)))
```

A Gaussian *generalised linear model* (GLM) relates a numeric outcome to a
covariate. Fit last hemoglobin as the outcome and first hemoglobin as the
covariate, both in g/dL. The servers keep the person-level data; the analyst sees
model coefficients and diagnostics.

```{r pooled-model}
pooled_model <- dsBaseClient::ds.glm(formula = "last ~ first", data = "D_pool_labs",
  family = "gaussian", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(pooled_model$coefficients)
```

The `first` coefficient is the fitted difference in last hemoglobin for a
one-g/dL difference in first hemoglobin. The intercept is the prediction at
first = 0, far outside these data. This small synthetic example teaches mechanics;
it is not a causal result or a validated prediction model.

<details><summary>Full model response</summary>

```{r pooled-model-full}
pooled_model
```

</details>

**Exercise 1.** Pool the first-value means. Would opening two connections to the
same 28 people be a valid way to increase the sample size?

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r pooled-exercise}
pooled_first <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_pool_labs$first", type = "combine", datasources = two_conns)
pooled_first
```

No. Duplicate people would be counted twice, and model uncertainty would be
misleading. These temporary sites instead have disjoint people.

</details>

**Exercise 2.** Is the mean of the two site medians necessarily the median you
would get from all 28 people together?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

No. These sites are equally sized, so `ds.quantileMean` averages their medians
equally. In general that differs from the median of the combined observations;
with unequal site sizes the function uses unequal weights.

</details>

## Recap

Start with a lab meaning and its recorded units. Choose the person-level reducer
and time window before releasing summaries. Declare conversions, distinguish
teaching cutoffs from recorded reference bounds, and distinguish change from
slope. Pool only compatible variables from nonoverlapping sites, and interpret
`ds.quantileMean` according to its weighted-site definition.

Close both practice sessions when finished; temporary split copies are removed.

```{r close-labs}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = two_conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(two_conns)
unlink(pair$path)
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
```
